(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-vignettes"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old woman taking warfarin for a mechanical valve presents with 2 days of sharp chest pain eased by leaning forward, widespread ST elevation and a small effusion. She is afebrile and her blood pressure is 128/78. Which is the most appropriate disposition?","choices":["Discharge with clinic follow-up","Discharge after one dose of ibuprofen","Outpatient cardiac imaging next week","Admit her to the hospital"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients on oral anticoagulants are among the higher-risk groups for acute pericarditis (with fever, subacute course, tamponade signs, large effusion, immunosuppression, trauma, raised troponin) and potentially need hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has heart-failure-like edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. The differential is narrowed to constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy. Which laboratory finding would favor constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["A markedly high natriuretic peptide","A normal sedimentation rate","Positive blood cultures","A relatively low natriuretic peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No B-type natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes constriction, but the level is likely lower in constrictive pericarditis than in restrictive cardiomyopathy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with typical acute pericarditis after a viral illness has a troponin that is above the reference range. Coronary angiography shows normal arteries. Which process does the troponin elevation most likely reflect?","choices":["A false-positive laboratory result","Myocardial involvement (myopericarditis)","An unrecognized coronary occlusion","Developing constrictive physiology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Troponin may be elevated if the myocardium is also affected, myopericarditis (perimyocarditis); an elevated troponin is also a feature that favors hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has typical acute viral pericarditis with stable vital signs and no high-risk features. A transthoracic echocardiogram is ordered. Which result is most expected?","choices":["A normal study or only a small effusion","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","A thick pericardium with septal bounce","Apical ballooning with a normal base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram is often normal in acute pericarditis unless there is an associated effusion, and the chest radiograph is typically normal; the diagnosis is primarily clinical. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman develops fever and positional chest pain with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) 2 weeks after a myocardial infarction treated with a coronary stent. Echocardiography shows a small effusion and her vital signs are stable. Which anti-inflammatory regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Aspirin plus colchicine","Indomethacin plus colchicine","Prednisone plus colchicine","Ibuprofen plus colchicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis after a myocardial infarction (Dressler syndrome) is treated with aspirin as the anti-inflammatory plus colchicine; the other nonsteroidal drugs and glucocorticoids are avoided after infarction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with acute pericarditis says the pain keeps him awake because it is worse when he lies down. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Sleep prone to splint the chest","Take an extra dose at bedtime","Keep the head of the bed elevated","Sleep flat to rest the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial pain is worse supine and better leaning forward, so keeping the head of the bed elevated is advised, along with completing the full treatment course. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a new inferior myocardial infarction involving the right ventricle is hypotensive with distended jugular veins and clear lungs. Tamponade is considered. Why must his infarction stay on the differential?","choices":["It excludes tamponade completely","It mimics subacute tamponade instead","It is the commonest cause","It mimics acute tamponade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The differential of acute tamponade includes myocardial infarction (especially with right ventricular involvement), aortic dissection and massive pulmonary embolism, which can all mimic it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has 2 days of worsening dyspnea and chest discomfort. Blood pressure is 84/56, heart rate 122, jugular veins are distended, and heart sounds are muffled. Her blood pressure drops by 18 mmHg on inspiration (pulsus paradoxus, an inspiratory drop in blood pressure). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Acute pericarditis","Tension pneumothorax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension, distended jugular veins and muffled heart sounds (the Beck triad) with pulsus paradoxus and tachycardia in a patient with malignancy are cardiac tamponade, pericardial fluid under pressure compressing the heart. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man began having pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pericardial pain 8 weeks ago. Despite treatment the symptoms have never fully gone away, with no symptom-free interval. Which term best describes his course?","choices":["Chronic pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive disease","Incessant pericarditis","Recurrent pericarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms persisting beyond 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission define incessant pericarditis; past 3 months the label becomes chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man received chest radiation for lymphoma 20 years ago. He has 8 months of exertional dyspnea, fatigue, leg edema and ascites. His jugular venous pressure is elevated and shows the Kussmaul sign (it rises on inspiration instead of falling), and a pericardial knock (an early diastolic sound, slightly earlier than a third heart sound) is heard. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pericarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Infective endocarditis","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluid overload with low output, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock after prior radiation are the picture of constrictive pericarditis, scarring that limits filling. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion is hypotensive and tachycardic. His electrocardiogram shows small QRS complexes with low voltage (small amplitude complexes) whose height alternates from beat to beat. Which electrocardiogram finding is this?","choices":["Second-degree heart block","Diffuse PR depression","Electrical alternans","Ventricular bigeminy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Electrical alternans, alternating QRS amplitude as the heart swings in the fluid, is fairly specific for tamponade and appears with low voltage and tachycardia. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man becomes hypotensive and short of breath an hour after a catheter procedure perforates his heart. Echocardiogram shows only a small pericardial effusion, estimated at about 150 mL, with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which best explains why so little fluid has compromised him?","choices":["Chronic stretching of the sac","The echocardiogram underestimated a liter","The pericardium was already calcified","Rapid filling of an unstretched sac"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute effusions can produce tamponade with small amounts of fluid (under 200 mL) because the pericardium has no time to stretch, unlike chronic effusions that grow large over time. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with acute pericarditis has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 2 weeks. He has been symptom-free for 4 days, and his weekly C-reactive protein has just returned to normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop every medication today","Increase the anti-inflammatory","Switch over to prednisone","Begin tapering the anti-inflammatory"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With the C-reactive protein approach, tapering starts once the patient is symptom-free and the level has normalized; colchicine continues for about 3 months. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman is being treated for acute pericarditis with an anti-inflammatory and colchicine. She asks whether she may take brisk walks while recovering. Which heart rate ceiling should guide her activity?","choices":["Under 60 beats per minute","Under 100 beats per minute","Under 150 beats per minute","No limit if she feels well"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. During the restriction, strenuous activity is avoided and the maximal heart rate is kept under 100 beats per minute, for at least 1 month and until symptoms and C-reactive protein normalize. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease has a large pericardial effusion with stable blood pressure, and his blood urea nitrogen is 112 mg/dL. Which treatment addresses the cause?","choices":["Antituberculous therapy","Corticosteroids","Pericardiectomy","Dialysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treating the underlying disorder is key; uremic pericarditis, associated with a blood urea nitrogen above 60 mg/dL, is treated with dialysis, and further management follows the cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman is discharged on ibuprofen and colchicine for acute pericarditis. Which new symptom should prompt her to go straight to the emergency department rather than call for an appointment?","choices":["Lightheadedness with shortness of breath","Mild fatigue in the late afternoon","Pain that still eases on leaning forward","Mild indigestion after doses of ibuprofen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Red-flag symptoms for tamponade or worsening disease, such as dyspnea, lightheadedness or falling exercise tolerance from low cardiac output, need emergency evaluation rather than a routine visit. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with known malignant pericardial effusion has a blood pressure of 82/50, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds and pulsus paradoxus (a 16 mmHg drop in blood pressure with inspiration). Echocardiogram shows a large effusion with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Loop diuretic infusion","Expedient pericardiocentesis","Intravenous fluids alone","High-dose anti-inflammatories"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade; it relieves the pressure and the fluid is sent for diagnostic studies. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has long-standing constrictive pericarditis with marked edema and ascites despite diuretics. A computed tomography scan shows a thick, heavily calcified pericardium. Which treatment offers definitive relief?","choices":["Pericardiectomy","Pericardiocentesis","High-dose colchicine","Heart transplantation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis is often a surgical disease; pericardiectomy, removal of part or most of the pericardium, is the definitive treatment and gives a 5-year survival near 80%. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. His family asks what to expect. Which statement about his long-term outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Age does not influence the outcome of surgery","His prognosis is as good as a young patient's","Survival without treatment is usually normal","His age and comorbidities predict a worse outcome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Older age, worse functional class and comorbidities such as renal dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia are independent predictors of poor long-term outcome in constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, dry skin and a heart rate of 54. Echocardiogram shows a large pericardial effusion without chamber collapse. Which test best addresses the cause her features suggest?","choices":["Tuberculin skin test","Blood cultures","Thyroid function tests","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism (myxedema) is a metabolic cause of pericardial effusion, and thyroid function tests are part of the effusion workup; her symptoms point straight at it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a moderate-to-large pericardial effusion on echocardiogram with no hemodynamic compromise. Viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate pericardiectomy","Diagnostic pericardiocentesis","Reassure and discharge","Start empiric anakinra"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic pericardiocentesis is considered for a moderate-to-large effusion of unknown etiology, as well as during therapeutic drainage for tamponade and with suspected neoplastic, purulent or tuberculous disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has her second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius, C-reactive protein is 45 mg/L, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which therapy is most appropriate to add?","choices":["Azathioprine","Pericardiectomy","Anakinra","Intravenous immunoglobulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With an inflammatory phenotype (C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever, imaging inflammation) and a colchicine-resistant second recurrence of idiopathic disease, an interleukin-1 blocker such as anakinra or rilonacept is added and now used earlier. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has 3 days of sharp substernal chest pain that improves when she sits forward. Her vital signs are normal, and no rub is heard on careful auscultation. Which additional finding would complete the clinical diagnosis of acute pericarditis?","choices":["Widespread ST elevation on electrocardiogram","A troponin above the reference range","An elevated B-type natriuretic peptide","Pericardial calcification on radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs two of four criteria: sharp substernal chest pain, a friction rub, suggestive electrocardiogram changes (widespread ST elevation) and a new or worsening effusion. Her pain plus the electrocardiogram would make two. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 19, 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with suspected tamponade has distended jugular veins and a blood pressure of 88/60. He has leg edema, and drainage is being arranged. Which drug class should be avoided?","choices":["Local anesthetics","Antibiotics","Diuretics","Intravenous crystalloid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fluid resuscitation is given in tamponade to increase preload and prevent right ventricular collapse, so diuretics, which reduce preload, should be avoided before drainage. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a large pericardial effusion from metastatic cancer reports a hoarse voice, hiccups and difficulty swallowing. Which mechanism explains his hoarseness?","choices":["Superior vena cava obstruction","Recurrent laryngeal nerve impingement","Direct invasion of the vocal cords","Hypoglossal nerve compression"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large effusions can cause dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness, the hoarseness from impingement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman developed early signs of constriction 6 weeks after an episode of acute pericarditis. She is hemodynamically stable, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent pericardiocentesis","Observation with no treatment","A longer course of pericarditis drugs","Pericardiectomy within the week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early, hemodynamically stable constriction may get the acute pericarditis medicines (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and colchicine) for longer, 2 to 3 months, with diuretics for fluid overload; edema or late gadolinium enhancement on cardiac magnetic resonance suggests reversible inflammation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 49"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has acute pericarditis with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) and widespread ST elevation. Her clinician wants laboratory values that confirm inflammation and can guide the length of therapy. Which pair of tests is most appropriate?","choices":["C-reactive protein and sedimentation rate","D-dimer and B-type natriuretic peptide","Lipase and hepatic transaminases","Blood cultures and procalcitonin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The initial workup includes complete blood count, troponins, erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein; the C-reactive protein is checked at presentation and weekly to guide tapering. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with ovarian cancer has had three drainages for a recurrent pericardial effusion, and the latest echocardiogram shows a loculated collection behind the heart. Which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Pericardial window","Fourth needle tap","Thoracentesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pericardiectomy or pericardial window is performed for recurrent effusions and for loculated effusions, especially posterior ones that cannot be approached percutaneously. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with confirmed constrictive pericarditis has failed a 3-month trial of anti-inflammatories and diuretics and remains fluid overloaded. Which service performs the definitive procedure she needs?","choices":["Nephrology","Cardiothoracic surgery","Interventional radiology","Gastroenterology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Constriction refractory to medical therapy needs pericardiectomy, an operation performed by cardiothoracic surgery working with cardiology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. She had mantle-field radiotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma 25 years ago and is otherwise well. Which feature of her history most worsens her long-term prognosis?","choices":["Her prior chest radiation","Her female sex","Her lack of other illness","Her cured lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Etiology is an important determinant of survival in constrictive pericarditis, and prior ionizing radiation is associated with the worst survival rates. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
